THINGS GOT HEATED

Hackers attacked European supermarket fridges

Fred Tanneau/Getty Images

Boiled gelato was the plat du jour after hackers targeted fridges and freezer=
s at dozens of European supermarkets in the early morning hours yesterday. Th=
e defrosting cyberattacks had temperatures reaching 122 Fahrenheit in some gr=
ocery store refrigerators across Belgium, Estonia, Finland, Ireland, Luxembou=
rg, the Netherlands, and Sweden.

A similar cyberattack in France last month had dozens of supermarkets dealing=
 with fires (on top of preemptively steamed broccoli), forcing them to toss m=
illions of euros worth of products.

How can this happen? The Belgian company Sabcobel, which operates some of the=
 fridges involved, said that hackers attacked a digital system that remotely =
controls the devices, putting them into defrost mode. Sabcobel said it had be=
en patching software vulnerabilities over the past few weeks but explained th=
at some fridges require physical modifications. Danfoss, a Danish refrigerati=
on temperature-control systems supplier, said its tech was targeted both in y=
esterday=E2=80=99s hack and in the earlier French cyberattack, affecting cust=
omers with non-updated software.

The US might have endured a similar incident. The Pentagon is investigating A=
ugust hacks on several military base commissary fridges that led to the mass =
melting of frozen foods.

Big picture: Fridges turning into saunas is heightening concerns about AI-ena=
bled hackers attacking critical infrastructure that relies on digital systems=
. More than 20 cyberattacks on refrigeration systems have taken place this ye=
ar, with some linked to Russia-aligned hackers, according to cybersecurity fi=
rm Forescout.=E2=80=94SK



