DUCK, DUCK, GOOSE

DC voters are being served a foie gras referendum

Niv Bavarsky

If the people of Washington, DC are tired of having this issue forced down th=
eir throats, imagine how the ducks feel=E2=80=94a referendum is on the ballot=
 in the nation=E2=80=99s capital today that would ban the sale of foie gras a=
long with the practice of overfeeding ducks through a metal tube that creates=
 the pricey dish.

The measure made it to the ballot due to the work of Pro-Animal Future, a lob=
bying group that spent nearly $400,000 and sent a cute duck to DC to sway vot=
ers (the group also got a similar measure onto the ballot in Denver). The gro=
up estimates that 26 DC-area restaurants serve foie gras, which is made from =
the fattened duck or goose liver.

The measure is expected to pass, but businesses are pushing back on the notio=
n that stuffing ducks right before they die is cruel and worry banning foods =
is a slippery slope. Critics also say the ban could unfairly affect suppliers=
. Hudson Valley Foie Gras, one of the two major US distributors, told Axios i=
t=E2=80=99s already planning a legal challenge..

Wrap it up: Even if voters say =E2=80=9Cget the duck out of here,=E2=80=9D th=
e ban wouldn=E2=80=99t take effect until July 2027. And it could be even long=
er: New York City voted in favor of a similar foie gras ban in 2019, but it r=
emains unenforced amid litigation.=E2=80=94DL

